this is alien where's all new area 51 a  gargantuan gaming PC with enough power  under its unique triad chassis to blaze  through even the most demanding of PC  games sporting one of Intel's Haswell  processors and x99 chipset along with  three of them videos gtx 980 graphics  cards the area 51 is all about  leading-edge technology  naturally that comes at a price the  Alienware comes in multiple  configurations for the one I'm testing  here is the most expensive coming in at  four thousand five hundred and forty  nine dollars it features and 1500 watt  power supply 256 gigabyte Samsung SSD a  4 terabyte hard drive 16 gigabytes of mm  133 megahertz ddr4 Ram intel core i7 59  30 K processor with a modest overclock  to 3.9 gigahertz and 3 NVIDIA GTX 980  graphics cards that's a hugely powerful  machine by any measure but it's worth  noting that you're paying quite a  premium for the case standing at over 22  inches tall over 9 inches wide and over  25 inches in depth  it's an imposing machine even by gaming  PC standards the Triad design does have  some practical benefits though for  starters both the rear and front panel  IO sit up at an angle which makes it  easier to plug into accessories and  displays you can also tilt the whole  thing forward for even easier access the  easily removable latch side panels  reveal a neat and tidy interior which  features two front intake fans one at  the top of the case and one near the  bottom the blows air towards the GPUs  the CPU is called by an all-in-one  liquid cooler with 120 mm radiator and  fans set to exhaust at the top the case  all of the drives including the slim  blu-ray writer are located behind the  motherboard with mounts available for 2  SSD  and a further three hard drives even if  you don't configure the system with all  those drives in place Alienware pre  mounts the cables you need to hook them  up which is a nice touch  sadly the drive bays aren't tool free so  you'll need to dig out some screws and a  screwdriver to get them in and out  similarly disappointing are the modular  cables which are your basic mismatched  OAM set and again given the cost is odd  not to see a more aesthetically pleasing  and sturdy a set of wrapped cables  inside the internal design does have its  benefits for airflow though by putting  the drives behind the motherboard tray  air can travel unobstructed over the  GPUs and CPU cooler idle CPU  temperatures average just below 40  degrees centigrade and under load rarely  pushed over 60  with three gtx 980m haswell-e  on board there's no surprise at the area  51 breezes through even the most  demanding of games at 1080p and 1440p at  4k in this case using invidious dynamic  super resolution technology the system  just the prank pushes over 60 frames per  second in most games but it still  couldn't quite manage the same with  Metro last light and Crysis 3 clearly 4k  at a consistent 60 frames per second is  still out of reach for this GPU  generation even with three cards in tow  so there's no arguing with the  performance of the area 51 for the most  part of what else would you expect from  cream-of-the-crop PC components this is  a supremely powerful machine one that  will be just as good for video editing  and 3d rendering work as it would for  playing the latest shooter annoyingly  even on cheaper versions of the machine  you can't spec it out within videos  cheaper GTX 970 GPUs which would shave  hundreds of dollars off the price you  either have to pick the pricey gtx 980  or go with older cards like the gtx 770  or 780 quite why the 970 isn't an option  is a mystery so while the area 51's case  may be to your tastes as usual the smart  option is to build your own PC but only  can you save several hundred dollars but  you can actually build a more powerful  computer too still if you must go  pre-built the area 51 is a well designed  quiet machine for the price that  compares favorably with systems from the  likes of origin and digital storm and as  such is one of the best pre-built  options out there
 

